{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"#3182","artifact_type":"_dh-work-item-head-v1","name":"head"},"owner_reference":"#3182","content":"{\"version\":1,\"issue_reference\":\"#3182\",\"parent_revision\":\"055de111fb936a4a6e4d9ae95058e8b2cbb3a1dd\",\"root_revision\":\"8d6d511f9b31c922774f61a3c27842d4c91609ad668155452ee6bb985b79edc9\",\"body\":\"<!-- backlog-metadata:\\npriority: P1\\ntype: Bug\\nstatus: open\\nadded: 2026-08-23\\n-->\\n\\n## Description\\n\\n`backlog_add` can complete with `item_ref: \\\"\\\"` \u2014 no GitHub issue created \u2014 while returning a success-shaped result. The caller receives a populated dict and no error.\\n\\nObserved in CI on `main`, run 32624257966, job \\\"Python / E2E live tests\\\":\\n\\n```\\nplugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py:196: in test_l1_add_with_real_issue\\n    assert item_ref.startswith(\\\"#\\\"), f\\\"Expected item_ref like '#N', got: {item_ref!r}\\\"\\nE   AssertionError: Expected item_ref like '#N', got: ''\\n\\nplugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py:318: in test_l11_resolve_alternative_end\\n    l11_issue_num = int(create_result[\\\"item_ref\\\"].lstrip(\\\"#\\\"))\\nE   ValueError: invalid literal for int() with base 10: ''\\n```\\n\\nTwo symptoms, one defect. The remaining lifecycle tests then skip on the `l1_ok` sentinel, and two more pass only because they assert types rather than values.\\n\\nWhy the cause is still unknown, which is the substance of this item: `operations._try_create_github_issue` (around line 1377) returns `None` on either `try_get_github(...) is None` (\\\"GitHub unavailable\\\") or a caught `GithubException`/`BacklogError` (\\\"Issue creation failed: {e}\\\"). Both are recorded only via `out.warn(...)`. The reason therefore lives in `result[\\\"warnings\\\"]`, and neither test assertion includes it, so roughly four weeks of CI logs contain no information about why creation failed.\\n\\nThis is the failure shape `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` prohibits directly: a write operation that does not report what changed, and a branch on an input condition whose fallback neither acts nor errors. An agent calling `backlog_add` cannot distinguish \\\"issue created\\\" from \\\"issue not created, item exists locally only\\\".\\n\\nNot caused by any recent change. Verified independently: every Code Quality run on `main` back through 2026-08-20 failed, including commits well before the recent backlog_core work (58df37b85, 79962289b, d22c524e6, 0a2ca9177). A subagent sampled e2e job conclusions on `main` on 2026-07-28, 08-06, 08-10 through 08-13, and every run from 08-14 to 08-23 \u2014 all failure, zero successes, so at least four weeks.\\n\\nThe symptom text changed once without the defect changing. Before 2026-08-18 it read `Expected item_ref in result, got: [...]` with `'reference': 'p1-mcp-test-<uuid>-live-test-item'` \u2014 the local-only fallback. PR #3160 made `item_ref` always present, so it became `got: ''`. Same bug throughout.\\n\\nRuled out as causes, with evidence: not a flake (20+ consecutive identical failures; the fixture uses a fresh tmp dir, a per-run UUID prefix and `force=True`); not rate limiting or auth rejection (step completes in 12.4s, no 403 or 429); not leftover state; not run contention. The job's token carries `Issues: write`.\\n\\nUser-provided context: a subagent's suggested cheapest first move is to include `result[\\\"warnings\\\"]` and `result[\\\"errors\\\"]` in the test assertion message, so the next CI run reports the actual cause \u2014 but that is a diagnostic aid, and the defect to fix is that a failed create is not surfaced to the caller at all. Whether the right contract is raising, an explicit status field, or something else is design work for grooming.\\n\\n## Fact-Check\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-04T00:49:34.188985Z</sub>\\n\\n\\n**verdict:** VERIFIED\\n\\n**Claim 1: `_try_create_github_issue` returns `None` on `try_get_github(...) is None` or caught exception, recorded only via `out.warn(...)`**\\n- **evidence:** operations.py lines 1444\u20131463 show `_try_create_github_issue` returns `None` on line 1458 (`try_get_github` returning `None`) and line 1463 (caught `GithubException`/`BacklogError`). Both failures record the reason to `out.warn()` only (lines 1457, 1462).\\n- **source:** `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1444\u20131463`\\n\\n**Claim 2: `_try_create_backend_issue_ref` converts that `None` into empty string `\\\"\\\"` with no separate error signal**\\n- **evidence:** operations.py line 1499 returns `f\\\"#{issue_num}\\\" if issue_num else \\\"\\\"`. When `_try_create_github_issue` returns `None` (line 1498), the expression evaluates to empty string. No error field or separate signal is populated in the return value.\\n- **source:** `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1498\u20131499`\\n\\n**Claim 3: `BacklogAddResponse` has unused `error: str | None` field designed for exactly this signal, left `None` on this failure path**\\n- **evidence:** tool_responses.py line 116 shows `FallibleToolResponse` base class declares `error: str | None = None`. BacklogAddResponse (line 212) inherits from FallibleToolResponse. Tracing the code path: `backlog_add` (server.py) calls `operations.add_item()`, which calls `_try_create_backend_issue_ref()` at line ~1390. The response is never populated with the error; failure only records warnings in `out` dict.\\n- **source:** `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tool_responses.py:116,212` and `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py` (pre-PR #3160 pattern where `backlog_add` returned `{\\\"error\\\": str(e)}` on BacklogError)\\n\\n**Claim 4: PR #3160 changed symptom text without changing defect; defect predates 2026-08-14 CI failures**\\n- **evidence:** PR #3160 (commit d837e171e, merged 2026-08-23) changed test assertions in test_live_validation.py: before the change, the test checked for `issue_num` field; after, it checks `item_ref.startswith(\\\"#\\\")`. The change only modified how the test reads the response, not the underlying `_try_create_github_issue`/`_try_create_backend_issue_ref` functions\u2014both are identical before and after PR #3160. Commit 765262e89 (Aug 12, 2026) introduced the `item_ref` field and changed response shape, predating PR #3160 and the reported CI failures (~Aug 14 onward). The defect (_try_create_backend_issue_ref returning empty string on failure without populating error) existed at or before commit 765262e89.\\n- **source:** `git log --oneline | grep \\\"3160\\\\|765262e89\\\"` and `git show d837e171e^:operations.py` vs. `git show d837e171e:operations.py` (functions identical)\\n\\n**Claim 5: Failure violates `rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` rule**\\n- **evidence:** rules/silent-failure-prevention.md section \\\"Branching on Input Values Requires an Explicit Fallback\\\" (lines 40\u201367) states: \\\"Every `if`/`elif` chain or match on an input value must have a final branch that either acts or errors. Falling through to `return unchanged` without logging is a silent data loss bug. If no fallback action is possible, raise or warn.\\\" The path `_try_create_backend_issue_ref` \u2192 `_try_create_github_issue` returns `None` on failure with only `out.warn()` (which the caller can ignore), then converts to empty string `\\\"\\\"` with no error signal to the MCP caller. The caller (test, agent, CLI) receives a success-shaped response with `item_ref: \\\"\\\"` and cannot distinguish \\\"issue created\\\" from \\\"issue not created.\\\" This violates the rule's requirement for an explicit fallback that \\\"either acts or errors.\\\"\\n- **source:** `/Users/jamienelson/repos/claude_skills/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md:40\u201367`\\n\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## RT-ICA\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-04T00:44:22.114047Z</sub>\\n\\nRT-ICA Snapshot: fix: backlog_add returns an empty item_ref when issue creation fails, and the reason reaches no caller\\nDate: 2026-09-04\\nGoal: Make backlog_add surface why issue creation failed instead of returning a success-shaped empty item_ref.\\nConditions:\\n1. Defect is still present in current code \u2014 `_try_create_github_issue`/`_try_create_backend_issue_ref` (operations.py:1444-1499) record failure only via `out.warn()` | Status: AVAILABLE\\n2. `BacklogAddResponse` already has an unused `error: str | None` field (tool_responses.py:216) designed for exactly this signal, left `None` on this path | Status: AVAILABLE\\n3. Repo convention for this exact shape is documented in rules/silent-failure-prevention.md: \\\"if no fallback action is possible, raise or warn\\\" | Status: AVAILABLE\\n4. Blast radius of populating `error`/changing the empty-string contract \u2014 which CLI, MCP, and test callers key off `item_ref == \\\"\\\"` today | Status: DERIVABLE\\n5. Whether the beads-backend sibling path (string-ID branch of `_try_create_backend_issue_ref`) shares the identical silent-failure shape | Status: DERIVABLE\\nAVAILABLE count: 3\\nDERIVABLE count: 2\\nMISSING count: 0\\n\\n</div>\\n\\n## Impact Radius\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-04T00:50:39.192670Z</sub>\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Producers**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1444` `_try_create_github_issue()` \u2014 returns `None` on either `try_get_github()` returning `None` (\\\"GitHub unavailable\\\") or a caught `GithubException`/`BacklogError`, recording the reason only via `out.warn()`. This is the source of the lost reason string.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1466` `_try_create_backend_issue_ref()` \u2014 dispatches beads vs. integer-ID backends; on the integer-ID path it collapses `_try_create_github_issue`'s `None` to `\\\"\\\"`, discarding the reason a second time. Docstring at 1476-1485 already documents the empty-string contract in detail.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/operations.py:1528` `add_item()` \u2014 builds the result dict (`title`, `priority`, `reference`, `file_path`, `item_ref`) at lines 1626-1636; there is no `error` key on this success-shaped return, only `out.to_dict()`'s `messages`/`warnings`/`errors`, which the MCP layer keeps separate from the typed `error` field (see server.py below). Any fix touches this dict-construction site.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/tool_responses.py:212` `BacklogAddResponse` \u2014 inherits `error: str | None` from `FallibleToolResponse` (tool_responses.py:113-117). This field is the \\\"already AVAILABLE\\\" fix surface named in RT-ICA condition 2: it is populated only in server.py's `except BacklogError` arm, never on the not-raised, empty-`item_ref` path.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/server.py:1377-1405` MCP tool `backlog_add` \u2014 calls `operations.add_item` then `_respond(BacklogAddResponse, {**result, **out.to_dict()})`; `error` is left unset here because no exception is raised for this failure mode, so the typed response's own error channel goes unused exactly on the path that needs it.\\n- `plugins/development-harness/sam_schema/backlog.py:38-73` CLI `backlog add` (Typer) \u2014 calls `operations.add_item` directly and re-emits via `_emit()`, which prints `output.warnings` to stderr but still exits 0 with `item_ref: \\\"\\\"` in the JSON payload. Same blind spot, second call site \u2014 a fix in `add_item`/`_try_create_backend_issue_ref` alone covers both the MCP tool and this CLI command since they share the one function.\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Consumers**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md:156-192` \\\"Step 5: Use `item_ref` from the response\\\" \u2014 the one workflow doc that programmatically consumes `backlog_add`'s return contract (\\\"The `backlog_add` response contains `item_ref` \u2014 use that value directly for all downstream workflows\\\"). It has no branch for the empty-`item_ref`/failure case today; whatever contract shape grooming picks (raise, explicit status, populate `error`), this step needs a corresponding \\\"if creation failed\\\" branch.\\n- No other skill, agent, or hook reads `result[\\\"item_ref\\\"]` or `BacklogAddResponse.error` from `backlog_add`/`add_item` \u2014 confirmed by tracing every non-test call site of `add_item` (CLI and MCP tool above are the only two production callers).\\n\\n**Code \u2014 Other References (naming collision, not in scope)**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/models.py:573` \u2014 `ContentDuplicateMatch.item_ref`, used in duplicate-detection error messages (`_check_for_duplicates`). Same field name, unrelated concern (an existing item's reference shown in a \\\"possible duplicate\\\" message, not `add_item`'s return value). Several agent/skill docs (e.g. `agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`) also use `item_ref` purely as a generic \\\"selector\\\" parameter name in their own prompts \u2014 unrelated to this contract. Flagging so grooming/planning doesn't chase these as callers.\\n\\n**Documentation**\\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/start.md` \u2014 see Consumers above; the only doc that specifies the response contract's consumption pattern. Needs a matching update if the contract shape changes.\\n- No other file under `plugins/development-harness/docs/` or `backlog_core/ARCHITECTURE.md` documents the `add_item`/`backlog_add` return contract itself (ARCHITECTURE.md:404 only documents the unrelated `ContentDuplicateMatch.item_ref`).\\n\\n**Configuration/CI**\\n- No CI workflow YAML, label config, or milestone config references this contract directly. The relevant CI surface is behavioral, not configuration: the \\\"Python / E2E live tests\\\" job (GitHub Actions) runs `test_live_validation.py`, whose `test_l1_add_with_real_issue` (line 196) and `test_l11_resolve_alternative_end` (line 318) are the two failing assertions named in the item description, plus downstream lifecycle tests that skip on the `l1_ok` sentinel. Fixing the contract should make these tests either pass or fail with an informative reason \u2014 no CI file edits are needed for the fix itself, only for the test assertions in `test_live_validation.py`.\\n\\n**Agent Instructions**\\n- No agent instruction file (`plugins/development-harness/agents/*.md`) branches on `backlog_add`'s `item_ref` or `error` fields. The apparent hits for `item_ref` in `agents/backlog-item-groomer.md`, `agents/classifier.md`, `agents/fact-checker.md`, `agents/rtica-assessor.md`, `agents/technical-researcher.md` are all the unrelated generic-selector usage described above (confirmed by reading each hit in context) \u2014 none of them consume the `add_item`/`backlog_add` return value.\\n- `rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` \u2014 not agent instructions to change, but the standing convention this defect violates (\\\"if no fallback action is possible, raise or warn\\\"); grooming/planning should re-read it when choosing the fix's exact contract shape (raise vs. explicit `error` field vs. something else), since the item explicitly leaves that design decision open.\\n\\n**Systems Inventory**\\n- Backend abstraction: only the integer-ID path (`_try_create_github_issue` \u2192 GitHub, and by extension sqlite/memory backends that share `_try_create_backend_issue_ref`'s integer-ID branch) exhibits the silent-failure shape. The beads (string-ID) sibling path already raises `ContentUnavailableError` from `BeadsBackend.put_work_item` when `create_beads_issue_for_item` fails \u2014 see Tests below. This resolves RT-ICA condition 5: the shape is **not** shared across backends; only the GitHub/sqlite/memory branch needs fixing.\\n- Two production call sites of `add_item`: the MCP tool `backlog_add` (server.py) and the CLI command `backlog add` (sam_schema/backlog.py). Both inherit whatever fix lands in `operations.add_item`/`_try_create_backend_issue_ref` \u2014 no separate fix needed per call site.\\n- Test suites touching this contract: `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_backlog_core_operations.py` (mocked unit tests, see below) and `plugins/development-harness/tests/test_live_validation.py` (the live E2E suite failing in CI).\\n\\n**Ecosystem Completeness Checklist**\\n- Producers: \u2705 found and enumerated (2 operations.py functions, 1 response model, 2 call sites).\\n- Consumers: \u2705 found (1 workflow doc); confirmed no other skill/agent/hook consumes the field.\\n- Tests: \u2705 found and consequential, not just discoverable \u2014\\n  - `test_backlog_core_operations.py:419-431` `test_add_item_item_ref_empty_when_no_github_issue` explicitly asserts `result[\\\"item_ref\\\"] == \\\"\\\"` as the *entire* signal on failure. This test codifies today's contract and **must be updated, not just supplemented**, by whatever fix grooming selects \u2014 it will start failing (or need reinterpretation) the moment `add_item` starts also raising/erroring on this path.\\n  - `test_backlog_core_operations.py:565-650` class `TestAddItemBeadsBackend` (3 tests) \u2014 proves the beads path already raises `ContentUnavailableError` rather than silently returning `\\\"\\\"`; use this as the target shape/precedent when the integer-ID path is fixed, and as evidence for RT-ICA condition 5 above.\\n  - `test_live_validation.py:196,318` \u2014 the originally failing CI assertions; the fix's acceptance criteria should include these going from opaque failure to either passing or raising/reporting the real reason.\\n- Documentation: \u2705 found (1 file); confirmed no broader doc surface.\\n- Configuration/CI: \u2705 checked; no config changes in radius, one named CI job is the observable surface.\\n- Agent instructions: \u2705 checked; none apply \u2014 naming collisions ruled out by reading each hit.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Design Intent Alignment\\n\\n<div><sub>2026-09-04T00:55:28.493550Z</sub>\\n\\n**Alignment assessment:** NOT_APPLICABLE\\n\\nThis is a pure defect fix to silent-failure behavior, not a feature proposal or architectural decision. The defect (backlog_add returning empty `item_ref` on GitHub issue creation failure with no error signal to the caller) directly violates the development-harness's standing `.claude/rules/silent-failure-prevention.md` convention\u2014specifically the rule that \\\"Every `if`/`elif` chain or match on an input value must have a final branch that either acts or errors\\\" (lines 40\u201367). \\n\\nFixing the violation aligns unambiguously with the product's mission: reliable, correct task management operations must report failures to their callers so that workflows (agents, CLI, MCP consumers) can respond appropriately. The fact-checked evidence shows:\\n- The beads backend already follows the correct pattern: it raises `ContentUnavailableError` on issue creation failure (test_backlog_core_operations.py, TestAddItemBeadsBackend)\\n- The GitHub/integer-ID backend violates the rule by returning empty string with no error signal\\n- The response model already has the `error` field designed for this signal (FallibleToolResponse inheritance)\\n\\nNo design tension exists between \\\"should a failed write operation report failure?\\\" and \\\"should it be silent?\\\" The answer for a production system is always the former. Grooming work is needed only on the *implementation detail*\u2014whether to raise, populate the `error` field, or use another mechanism\u2014not on whether to surface the failure.\\n</div>\\n\\n## Context\\n\\n<div><sub>0000-00-00T00:00:00Z</sub>\\n\\n- **Source**: Diagnosed from the E2E live tests failure on main, 2026-08-23\\n- **Priority**: P1\\n- **Added**: 2026-08-23\\n- **Research questions**: None\\n</div>\\n\",\"digest\":\"61260a9e5b3cc0c2733fa81de83c06ae3e356c54cfee1ab40449d2a61300357b\",\"comment_id\":\"IC_kwDOQGimCs8AAAABSdwNPw\"}"}